# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{lb|en|also|reflexive}} ''Followed by'' '''[[of]]''': to [[free#Verb|free]] (oneself or someone, or a [[place#Noun|place]]) from an [[annoyance]] or [[hindrance]].
##: {{synonyms|en|deliver|disencumber}}
##: {{ux|en|I’m glad to be '''rid''' of that stupid nickname.}}
##: {{ux|en|We’re trying to '''rid''' the world of poverty.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1872|author=Timothy D. Sullivan|quotee=attributed to {{w|Henry II of England}}|chapter=From the Accession of Henry, in 1554, to His Death in 1189|title=The Story of England. A Narrative of English History, from the Earliest Period to the Present Time.{{nb...|In Two Volumes.}}|location=Dublin|publisher=A. M. Sullivan|volume=!I (From the landing of Julius Cæsar to the Reign of Henry VIII)|page=116|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/storyofenglandna00sull/page/116/mode/1up|oclc=219827791|passage="A curse on the cowardly varlets I have about me," said the King {{quote-gloss|Henry II}}, "who have left me so long exposed to his {{quote-gloss|{{w|Thomas Becket}}'s}} insolence. Will no one '''rid''' me of this turbulent priest?"|footer={{small|The quotation was first phrased in this way in 1740, but is said to be an inaccurate statement of what the king said: see {{cite-book|author=w:Simon Schama|title=[[w:A History of Britain (book series)|A History of Britain: At the Edge of the World? 3000 BC&nbsp;– AD 1603]]|location=London|publisher=w:BBC Books|page=147|isbn=978-0-563-38497-7}}.}}}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|month=May|title=News and Comment: Minister Hamstrings BR Workshops|journal=w:Modern Railways|location=Shepperton, Surrey|publisher=w:Ian Allan Publishing|page=291|issn=0026-8356|oclc=884584817|passage=If the Government believes that part of the railways' salvation is to be found in '''ridding''' them of extraneous concerns, it should have had the courage either to close the railway works down as quickly as possible, or to hive them off as an entirely separate concern, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|date=9 March 2014|author=Jacob Steinberg|title=[[w:Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan]] shock [[w:Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] in FA Cup again to reach semi-finals|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230608064158/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/mar/09/manchester-city-wigan-athletic-fa-cup|passage=All the billions in the world and Manchester City still cannot '''rid''' themselves of the most persistent thorn in their side.}}
## {{lb|en|also|reflexive}} ''Followed by'' '''[[away]]''''','' '''[[from]]''''', or'' '''[[out of]]''': to remove (oneself or someone, or something, which is [[annoying#Adjective|annoying]], [[troublesome]], or [[unwanted]]).
##* {{quote-book|en|date=24 March 2010|author=John H. Goodwin|chapter=41|title=The Reluctant Spy|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=w:AuthorHouse|page=293|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=na3hhx_n_M0C&pg=PA293|isbn=978-1-4343-8193-4|passage=Worst of all though were the leeches. The soldiers had managed to '''rid''' them from the camp interiors, but once you ventured out on patrol and into the wetlands, they were everywhere.}}
## {{lb|en|now|only|_|reflexive}} ''Often followed by'' '''from''''','' '''of''''', or'' '''out of''': to [[free#Verb|free]] or [[release#Verb|release]], or to [[rescue#Verb|rescue]] or [[save#Verb|save]], (oneself or someone, or something).
## {{lb|en|archaic}}
### {{senseid|en|clear}} To [[clear#Verb|clear]] (a place or [[way#Noun|way]]); especially, to clear ([[land#Noun|land]]) of [[tree#Noun|trees]], [[undergrowth]], etc.
###* {{RQ:Camden Holland Britain|chapter=Romans in Britaine|pages=63–64|pageref=63|passage=The vvaies {{...}} [[w:Trajan|Trajanius]] repaired, by paving vvith stone, or raiſing vvith banks caſt up ſuch peeces of them as vvere moiſt and miry; by ſtocking up & '''ridding''' ſuch as vvere rough and overgrovven vvith buſhes and briers: {{...}}}}
### ''Sometimes followed by'' '''away''': to [[destroy]] or [[kill#Verb|kill]] (someone).
### ''Sometimes followed by'' '''[[up#Preposition|up]]''': to [[remove#Verb|remove]] [[obstacle]]s or [[refuse#Noun|refuse]] from (a place); to [[clean#Verb|clean]], to [[clear out]]; specifically, to clear or [[empty#Verb|empty]] (the [[stomach#Noun|stomach]]).
###* {{RQ:Hardy Pair of Blue Eyes|volume=II|chapter=To that Last Nothing under Earth|page=266|passage=Come, lads, drink up your ale, and we'll just '''rid''' this corner, so as to have all clear for beginning at the wall as soon as 'tis light to-morrow.}}
### {{lb|en|[[Cheshire]]}} ''Followed by'' '''up''': to [[eradicate]] (something); to [[root out]], to [[uproot#Verb|uproot]].
### {{lb|en|Shropshire}} ''Sometimes followed by'' '''away''' ''or'' '''[[off#Preposition|off]]''': to [[complete#Verb|complete]] or [[get through]] (a [[task#Noun|task]], or [[work#Noun|work]]).
## {{lb|en|chiefly|US|regional|rare}} ''Often followed by'' '''up''': to [[put#Verb|put]] (a place, such as a [[room#Noun|room]] or a [[table#Noun|table]]) [[in order#Adverb|in order]]; to clear, to [[tidy#Verb|tidy]].
##* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|volume=III|chapter=LII|page=197|passage=The four breakfasted by the thin light, and the ‘house-'''ridding'''’ was taken in hand.|footer=A noun use.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}}
### To [[dismiss]] (someone) who has [[attend]]ed on a [[person#Noun|person]]; {{lb|en|reflexive}} to remove (oneself) from a person's [[presence#Noun|presence]] after attending to them.
### To [[settle#Verb|settle]] (a [[disagreement]]).
### {{lb|en|also|reflexive}} ''Followed by'' '''of''': to [[deprive]] (oneself or someone) of something; to [[strip#Verb|strip]].
### {{lb|en|rare}} To [[get away#Verb|get away]] from or [[get out#Verb|get out]] of (a place); to [[escape#Verb|escape]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}}
### To clear land or some other place.
### {{lb|en|West Midlands}} To clear or empty the stomach; also, to [[clear one's throat|clear the throat]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete|Northern England}} Of work: to be completed.

